======================================================================
RE-TESTING the unrescued cases with L up to 60 (the cap above was too tight)
  re-tested            : 86
  cleared with L <= 60 : 86
  STILL none           : 0
  -> the 86 was a search-bound artefact, not a finding.
  full lattice distribution: {2: 3697, 3: 977, 4: 300, 5: 97, 6: 11, 7: 8}

READING, and what this does and does not establish.
  Every one of the 5,090 (runner, arrangement) pairs with no lonely instant on its own
  AP lattice L=1 HAS one on a higher lattice, and the lattice needed is small:
  L=2 for 73%, L<=3 for 92%, L<=7 for all of them. Maximum L needed: 7.

  So 'no lonely own-AP instant' at L=1 is not a failure to be isolated. It is the
  case where section 6.2's dilation does its job: the runner is pushed off the AP and
  the isolation is still achieved, at a lattice the framework names.

  SCOPE. 5,000 arrangements: 4,400 perturbations of the AP (1..8) with deviations up to
  0.45 of the gap across denominators 3..25, plus 600 random integer 8-sets from [1,200].
  Rational speeds only -- exact periodicity is what makes one period decisive. This is
  measurement over those families, NOT a proof that a lattice always carries the instant,
  and not a bound on L in general: the L<=7 here is observed, not derived.

  METHOD WARNING, recorded because it bit twice in this script. The first run capped L at
  floor(s_max/8)+2, read off the manuscript's FIRST-LIVE-LATTICE bound. That bound says
  which lattices are live, not how far a lonely instant may sit, and for s_max=7.5 it
  searches only L=2. It reported 86 failures. All 86 cleared once L ran to 60. A cap taken
  from the wrong quantity manufactures nulls.
